>>the age of a potion of oil from a bones file wasn't being handled correctly
>Huh?

Most objects have an age, but this is stored as the turn number when the
object was created, so that it's not necessary to increment it every turn.
However, this field is reused for light sources; instead, it stores
the number of turns of fuel remaining.

A consequence of the way age is stored is that ages of objects in bones
files must be changed by a number equal to the difference between the
number of turns played in the bones game and the number of turns played in
the current game - so that if I enter a bones file where a corpse was
killed on turn 13000 and the player died on 13010, and I have played 24000
turns, the corpse's "age" will be set to 23090.

This adjustment should not be performed for things where "age" is
remaining fuel, but was for potions of oil. Hence one could get potions of
oil with vast quantities of fuel - or none at all.
